<title>TUNISIA: Tunisia to use a $50 mln U.S. commodity credit.</title>
<headline>Tunisia to use a $50 mln U.S. commodity credit.</headline>
<dateline>TUNIS 1997-02-26</dateline>
<text>
<p>Tunisian parliament adopted a draft law which enables the country to utilise a $50 million U.S. credit line for commodity imports, including wheat purchases in 1997, a Tunisian official said on Wednesday.</p>
<p>&quot;The parliament adopted a draft law on Tuesday which will permit Tunisia to utilise a total $50 million U.S. credit line to finance imports of agricultural products this year,&quot; an official at the state-run Office des Cereals told Reuters.</p>
<p>It will cover vegetable oil, wheat, barley, and maize, the official said but gave no details. The deadline for the credit line was set for September 1997, the official added.</p>
<p>Plenty of rain during the month of January had made Tunisian farmers and agricuture officials optimistic about the cereals and olive crops during the next season.</p>
<p>Tunisia had an exceptional grain harvest of 2.87 million tonnes for the 1995/96 season, officials said.</p>
</text>
